// (1) SYNCHRONIZED METHOD: locks the whole method on `this`. Coarse.
public synchronized void increment() { count++; }
//   Equivalent to: synchronized(this) { count++; }
//   Problem: callers can also lock on `this` -> deadlock risk; long method
//   holds the lock a long time.

// (2) SYNCHRONIZED BLOCK: locks only the critical region on a PRIVATE lock.
private final Object lock = new Object();
public void increment() {
  // ... other non-critical work that DOESN'T need the lock ...
  synchronized (lock) {            // fine-grained, private lock
    count++;
  }
}
//   Benefits: only the critical region is locked; the lock is private so
//   callers can't lock on it -> safer; better throughput.
```
